1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,617
A forklift passes a worker.

2
00:00:01,667 --> 00:00:03,617
A crowd presses toward a gate.

3
00:00:03,667 --> 00:00:04,950
A queue backs up.

4
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,825
The cameras saw it all.

5
00:00:06,875 --> 00:00:07,950
Nobody was watching.

6
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,800
This is Rulecam.

7
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,450
A warehouse manager sets one rule, in Spanish:

8
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:16,950
keep people four metres from a moving forklift.

9
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,677
Muse Spark plans it, SAM 3.1 measures the floor, and Rule CI attacks it.

10
00:00:22,727 --> 00:00:25,950
Sixteen edge cases, six broken copies. All caught.

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,075
On live video, Muse Glimmer, Meta's open model on our own GPU, confirmed six near misses out of six.

12
00:00:33,125 --> 00:00:37,950
And it knew the seated driver was the operator, so nobody was bothered.

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,621
Spark writes the warning in Spanish.

14
00:00:40,671 --> 00:00:42,848
Meta's speech model says it.

15
00:00:42,898 --> 00:00:47,300
Voice Transcribe listens back, to check it was said right.

16
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,150
In a crowd crush, nobody does anything wrong.

17
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,950
People are packed too tightly to move.

18
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,037
This is real footage from a crowd experiment, with an instruction in Hindi.

19
00:01:01,087 --> 00:01:04,950
SAM 3.1's counts stayed within four percent of the truth.

20
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,550
Spark calls it critical.

21
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:16,950
The plan was to send people to side door B, but the route guard saw that door was filling too, so Spark held the inflow instead.

22
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,950
In one run, Glimmer confirmed in six seconds, and stewards were told twenty-six seconds after the reading.

23
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,950
When a Hindi announcement came back saying "crowd" instead of "gate", it was held back.

24
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,728
A shop owner asks:

25
00:01:31,778 --> 00:01:33,950
when was the queue longest?

26
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,158
The tracker said twenty-two people.

27
00:01:36,208 --> 00:01:42,783
Rulecam counted the same frame three ways, with SAM, Spark and Glimmer, and said ten.

28
00:01:42,833 --> 00:01:44,550
By hand: about twelve.

29
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,950
It spots a spill, tells the right person, and closes the task only when a photo shows the floor is clean.

30
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,550
Ask out loud in Spanish, and the answer comes back in Spanish.

31
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,521
Connect the cameras you already have.

32
00:02:07,571 --> 00:02:10,950
About thirty seconds to a named, surveyed camera.

33
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,950
Staff get the alert on their phone, by role, never by name, in their own language.

34
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,950
In glasses mode, ask what you're looking at, and hear the answer.

35
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:23,987
Why trust any of this?

36
00:02:24,037 --> 00:02:26,431
You don't have to trust us.

37
00:02:26,481 --> 00:02:32,950
An inspector opens a proof pack, and their own browser re-checks every record and every number.

38
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,950
Change one number, and it turns red.

39
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,303
Workers can dispute any alert, and a false alarm becomes a permanent test.

40
00:02:41,353 --> 00:02:42,950
No face recognition. Ever.

41
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,690
Need a new kind of rule?

42
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:49,800
Muse Code writes it, and a solver proves it.

43
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:51,767
Rulecam. Plain words in.

44
00:02:51,817 --> 00:02:57,217
Verified alerts out. On the cameras you already own, in any language.
